1.text:
For an air filled chamber

* W/e = 33.97 V for air

the power deposited in the air which is seen by the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}=6.8\times10^{-11}~{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is seen by the beam.  With the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.3\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm \;cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and a sensitive length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ along the beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}} .
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\;{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  Hence the
air-kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{{\dot E}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq 0.19~{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{t}\approx\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{a}$ the same
number is the absorbed dose rate in tissue in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

In terms of photon fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \dot \Phi\,E\,
\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big)_{a},
\qquad 
\dot\Phi=\frac{\dot D_{t}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi\approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-4}}
{5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J}\times0.0328{\rm m^{2}kg^{-1}}}
\approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV in the focal spot, in agreement with
$\dot D =I\,W/e/m $.

A material point of the object is in the focal spot for the dwell
time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\,,
\end{equation*}

where $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ is the focal width and $v_{s}$ the scan
speed.  The quoted

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure}}\equiv
\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=0.02~{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time per point.  Hence the dose delivered to a surface
element during one energy exposure is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;\mu{\rm rad}$ per energy in the
focal spot.  In photon terms this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot\Phi\,E\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big) t_{d}
     \approx 2.2\times10^{10}\ {\rm photons\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per 0.02 s dwell.

For the usual dual-energy, i.e. two–energy, acquisition the
cumulative surface dose is $\simeq2D_{1}\approx7$–$8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$,
about $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromator energy, essentially the
air-kerma rate $1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ integrated over the
0.02 s beam dwell time per pixel.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal demagnification of the Laue monochromator is already
contained in the measured $2.0\,{\rm pA}$ focal current.  This is the
$\sim 10^{-6}{\rm Gy}$ surface dose normally quoted for synchrotron
micro-beam multi-energy imaging.

3.text:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge liberated per unit time is

* $ {\rm d}Q/{\rm d}t=I$ ,   $N_{i}=I/e$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$

and the power put into the air in the volume which is actually
irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=N_{i}W=\frac{I\,W}{e}\;,
\end{equation*}
$W=33.97\;{\rm eV}$  per ion pair.  With $W_{J}=W\,e=5.44\times10^{-18}\,$J

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
in the volume which is seen by the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\,{\rm mm^{2}}
      =1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the chamber taken as the air column which is traversed by the
beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}
       =1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272=3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
       =3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
in the beam.  The air–kerma rate in the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \simeq 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.19~{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ of air kerma in the 0.30$\times$6
mm$^{2}$ microbeam.  With $\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{t}\simeq
\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{air}$ the same number is the tissue-kerma rate
in the microbeam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
 \approx0.69\;{\rm Gy\,h^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the stationary beam.

A material point of the object is under the 0.30 mm wide microbeam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
per pass, the quantity quoted in the statement.  The dose actually
deposited in a point which is traversed once by the scanning microbeam
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{pt}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\approx
3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per dwell, i.e. $\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$
per 0.02 s exposure in the 33 keV Laue microbeam.  This is the
instantaneous entrance skin kerma in the beam; the quoted
$1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ is the conventional $0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air–kerma rate at the focus.

In the usual way the number is quoted as an instantaneous rate.  On the
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field which is covered by the scan the
integrated energy put into the whole field per complete scan is
$K_{pt}A_{mono}/A_{f}$.  With $A_{mono}/A_{f}=50/0.30=1.67\times10^{2}$ this
is

\begin{equation*}
K_{tot}\simeq K_{pt}\frac{A_{mono}}{A_{f}}\approx
3.9\times10^{-6}\times1.67\times10^{2}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ of energy deposited in the 50 mm wide
field for one complete single–energy scan, the $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per point being the conventional entrance–surface dose quoted for
synchrotron microbeam multiple–energy imaging.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\times
6\,{\rm mm}$ incident size and the $0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ 
$\mu_{en}/\rho$ cancel in the kerma–rate expression, as expected, and
the quoted $\,t_{d}=0.02\;{\rm s}$ is the dwell time of a tissue point
under the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide microbeam.

7.text:
The air kerma rate which can be read off the ion chamber is

* $I=2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}$, $W_{air}\approx 33.97\;{\rm J/C}$,

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W_{air}
\end{equation*}

and for the air which is actually seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\ {\rm cm^{2}},\qquad
V_{f}=A_{f}L ,
\end{equation*}

$L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ is the length of the chamber along the beam.  With
$\rho_{air}=1.293\;{\rm mg\;cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\ {\rm cm^{2}}\times
15.1\ {\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

the air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W_{air}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}  ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\;h^{-1}}$ in air at the focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the absorbed-dose rate
in soft tissue at the focus is the same to a very good approximation,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $33\;{\rm keV}$ monochromatic beam.

In the scanning geometry a tissue element is illuminated only while the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beamlet passes over it.  With the quoted
$w_{f}/v$ dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx\frac{w_{f}}{v}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dose actually deposited in the element in one passage through the
beamlet is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of the order of a few $\mu$Gy per monochromatic exposure.  With
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm V}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\frac{I\,W_{air}\,t_{d}}{ \rho_{air}A_{f}L}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\ \mu{\rm Gy\ per\;33\,keV\;pass}
\end{equation*}

in soft tissue, essentially the same as the air kerma delivered to the
surface.  In the usual dual- or triple-energy K-edge scheme the doses add
incoherently, so a two-energy acquisition is $\approx 2D_{1}\approx
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ and a three-energy acquisition is
$\approx 3D_{1}\approx 1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$ to the surface
that is scanned.  The often quoted $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV
micro-beam exposure is the conventional single-energy figure.

18.text:
The charge collected from the air in the chamber is a measure of the energy
which is absorbed in the air in the chamber.

For an air kerma

\begin{equation*}
K_{air}= \frac{Q}{m}\,\frac{W}{e}\approx
\frac{I}{m}\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

with $W/e\simeq 33.97\,{\rm V}$ for air. The air mass which is
in the beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}~A_{f}L ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\qquad L=15.1\;{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
0.018\times15.1\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

The power absorbed in the air in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}= I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W},
\end{equation*}

so that the peak air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{P_{a}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{air}A_{f}L}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times 6\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot.  In terms of an energy
fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}=\frac{P_{a}}{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}m_{air}}
          \approx5.9\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\Phi_{ph}\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at
33 keV, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$,
$E_{ph}=5.29\times10^{-15}\,{\rm J}$.

For tissue $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the
dose rate at the surface in the focal spot is essentially the air
kerma rate above.

A point on the surface is seen by the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide
focal spot for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
      \approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the $w_{f}/(\Delta x/\Delta t)$ which is quoted.  Hence the dose
deposited in a surface element during one pass of the monochromatic
beam is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq \dot K_{air}\,t_{d}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic projection, i.e. $\,\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV
exposure, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak in the focal spot.
In energy fluence terms this is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{E,1}= \dot\Phi_{E}t_{d}\approx1.2\times10^{-8}\;{\rm J\,cm^{-2}}
\approx2.2\times10^{6}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per pass.  For an $n$-energy multiple–energy acquisition the
cumulative surface dose is $n\,D_{1}$, $\sim 8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
typical dual–energy acquisition and $\sim12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for three
energies, essentially the $3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy often
quoted for synchrotron scanning micro–CT.  This is the peak surface
dose; the mean dose over the scanned area is lower by the
$w_{f}/\Delta x$ overlap factor.

12.text:
For an air walled ion chamber

\begin{equation*}
{\dot H}_{air}= \frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}=\frac{I}{e}\frac{W}{e^{-1}}/
m_{air}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate in the volume which is intercepted by the beam.
The volume which is intercepted when the chamber is on the focus is

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=w_{f}h_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}=3.0\times10^{-2}{\rm cm}\qquad 
h_{f}=6.0\;{\rm mm}=6.0\times10^{-1}{\rm cm}\qquad 
L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.03\times0.60\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=V_{b}\rho_{air}=0.272\times1.293\times10^{-3}
       =3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg} 
\end{equation*}

in the beam.  With the air  $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$,

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=\frac{I\,W}{e}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
       =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm \;W}
\end{equation*}

so that the instantaneous air kerma rate on the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}
         =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
         \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.019\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e.  about $ 1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy/s}$ in the 0.3$\times$6 mm
spot.  In the thin air limit

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{tissue}\simeq\dot K_{air}\;, \qquad 
\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big|_{tissue}\simeq\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big|_{air},
\end{equation*}

so the entrance kerma rate in tissue is the same as the air kerma rate
in the chamber.

The sample is rastered horizontally.  The time a material point
spends inside the $w_{f}$ wide spot is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/{\rm exposure}}
\end{equation*}

and the data are given as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=0.020\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

i.e. the conventional dwell time of a pixel in the 0.30 mm spot.
The kerma received by a material point during one 33 keV pass is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{tissue}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic exposure, i.e. of the order

\[
K_{1}\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}\approx3.9\times10^{-4}{\rm rad}
\]

entrance kerma at the surface for the 33 keV projection.  In air
kerma units this is $ \approx 3.9\times10^{-6}$ J kg$^{-1}$ per
exposure.  With the usual $W_{air}=33.97$ eV this is the $\,I\,W/e$
energy deposited in the $0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}$ of air in the beam
during $t_{d}$.

For a multi-energy set the surface kerma adds incoherently.  For e.g.
$N_{E}$ energies of comparable flux the cumulative entrance kerma is
$K_{\rm cum}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}$, i.e. $\sim 8$–$12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the
two- to three-energy acquisitions normally used in the Laue
microprobe.  This is the familiar $\mu$Gy–level synchrotron
micro-tomography entrance dose.  The time-averaged dose over the
$w_{M}\times h_{M}=50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field which is scanned
with the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ spot is $K_{1}w_{f}/w_{M}\approx
2.3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per pass, as usual for a raster-scanned
microbeam.

10.text:
For an air ionisation chamber the energy which is put into the air is

* $E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e$

with $W/e\simeq 33.9\;{\rm J/C}$ for air.  For the measurement quoted

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\,{\rm J/C}
      \simeq 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}
in the air which is actually intersected by the beam.  With the beam
$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ $h_{f}=6.0\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus the air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=w_{f}h_{f}L=
0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g/cm^{3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
      \simeq 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate which is produced in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}w_{f}h_{f}L}
      \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ this is the
tissue-kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ pencil beam.
In the usual scanning notation the dwell time of a material point in the
pencil is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/\Delta t}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually received by a material point on a single
Laue channel is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.020
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\,{\rm \sim 3\!-\!4\;\mu Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$ exposure,
$\,\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous kerma in the beam.
This is the conventional ‘single-energy’ synchrotron pencil-beam dose
often quoted for the system.

If the dose is summed over the whole Laue fan which is scanned in the
usual multiple-energy mode the $50\,{\rm mm}$ source width is imaged
onto the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus.  The number of independent Laue
channels which are stepped through in a complete horizontal scan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the total kerma delivered to a surface element which is taken
through the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ fan is

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm tot}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx
1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative surface kerma for the
$\approx 3.3\;{\rm s}$ $50\;{\rm mm}$ multi-energy scan, i.e. $\approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per Laue energy step, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous in the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm}$ beam.  The $3.9\;\mu{\rm
Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell is the usual single-channel figure which
is quoted as $\,\sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per image.  With the often quoted
$W/e=33.85\;{\rm J/C}$ the numbers become $K_{1}\approx3.86\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
and $K_{\rm tot}\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ for the full 167-channel fan,
essentially the same result.

16.text:
For an air filled ionisation chamber the charge which is liberated per
unit absorbed energy is $e/W_{air}$ with

* $W_{air}/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$

so that the power which is deposited in the gas in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{W_{air}}{e}I .
\end{equation*}

With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =33.97\times2.0\times10^{-12}=6.79\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is the power which is absorbed in the air which is at any instant in
the 33.0 keV focal spot.

The air which is in the spot at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}= w_{f}h_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}=3.0\times10^{-2}{\rm cm}$,
$h_{f}=6.0\,{\rm mm}=6.0\times10^{-1}{\rm cm}$ and
$L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$, so that

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.018\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

and with $\rho_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{b}=\rho_{air}V_{b}=3.5\!-\!3.6\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the focal spot.  The air kerma rate in
the spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{b}}
          =\frac{I\,W_{air}/e}{m_{b}}
          \simeq\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-4}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.19\;\mu{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$, an energy–absorption
mass–attenuation coefficient of air of $0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at
33 keV being essentially that of soft tissue.  The photon fluence rate
which corresponds to this kerma rate is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{air}}{E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}
      \approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-7}}
                   {5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.328}
      \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in the focal spot.

A material point is in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
      \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step/exposure}}
      \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}

the time quoted in the statement.  The air kerma, and to a very good
approximation the absorbed dose in tissue, received by a point which
passes once through the spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}\;t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-7}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-9}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per single–energy projection, i.e. a few nGy per projection, $ \approx
3.9\,{\rm nGy}$ for the numbers quoted.  In energy-fluence terms this is

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{1}= \Phi t_{d}E\approx1.2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per projection, $K_{1}= \Psi_{1}\mu_{en}/\rho\approx3.9\times10^{-9}\ {\rm
Gy}$.  For the two or three energies normally used in a Laue multiple-
energy tomography the dose adds linearly, e.g. $\approx 7\!-\!12\,{\rm
nGy}$ for two–three energies.

If the dose is referred to the $50\,{\rm mm}$ entrance field which is
imaged by scanning the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
N_{p}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

steps are required.  The integrated energy deposited in the whole
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per single-energy scan is

\begin{equation*}
K_{field}\approx N_{p}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-9}
          \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, essentially the average kerma which is delivered to the
scanned tissue.  The $4\,{\rm nGy}$ per projection is the conventional
single-point cumulative surface dose; the $\sim0.6\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ 
figure is the integrated dose over the 50 mm field of view which is
normally quoted as the cumulative surface dose of the scan.  The
$\sim2\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ figure often quoted after the $L_{c}/w_{f}\approx
503$ averaging correction for the 15.1 cm scanning ion chamber is
$K_{1}^{\rm pk}\approx1.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy projection, i.e.
$\approx 1\!-\!2\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection for the usual 2–3 energy
set, the few-nGy number above being the dose per point in the
un-corrected, stationary-chamber limit.

public_text.1:
For an air filled chamber

* W/e = 33.97 V for air

the power deposited in the air which is seen by the chamber is

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=I\,\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
{\dot E}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}=6.8\times10^{-11}~{\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is seen by the beam.  With the beam at the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.3\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm \;cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

and a sensitive length $L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ along the beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}} .
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\;{\rm mg\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
      \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is ionised at any instant.  Hence the
air-kerma rate in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{{\dot E}}{m_{a}}=\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{a}}
          \approx\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\simeq 0.19~{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{t}\approx\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{a}$ the same
number is the absorbed dose rate in tissue in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

In terms of photon fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}= \dot \Phi\,E\,
\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big)_{a},
\qquad 
\dot\Phi=\frac{\dot D_{t}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi\approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-4}}
{5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J}\times0.0328{\rm m^{2}kg^{-1}}}
\approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}
\approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV in the focal spot, in agreement with
$\dot D =I\,W/e/m $.

A material point of the object is in the focal spot for the dwell
time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\,,
\end{equation*}

where $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ is the focal width and $v_{s}$ the scan
speed.  The quoted

\begin{equation*}
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step}/{\rm exposure}}\equiv
\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=0.02~{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

is the dwell time per point.  Hence the dose delivered to a surface
element during one energy exposure is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
     \approx3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;\mu{\rm rad}$ per energy in the
focal spot.  In photon terms this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot\Phi\,E\Big(\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big) t_{d}
     \approx 2.2\times10^{10}\ {\rm photons\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per 0.02 s dwell.

For the usual dual-energy, i.e. two–energy, acquisition the
cumulative surface dose is $\simeq2D_{1}\approx7$–$8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$,
about $4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromator energy, essentially the
air-kerma rate $1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ integrated over the
0.02 s beam dwell time per pixel.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal demagnification of the Laue monochromator is already
contained in the measured $2.0\,{\rm pA}$ focal current.  This is the
$\sim 10^{-6}{\rm Gy}$ surface dose normally quoted for synchrotron
micro-beam multi-energy imaging.

public_text.3:
In an air ionisation chamber the charge liberated per unit time is

* $ {\rm d}Q/{\rm d}t=I$ ,   $N_{i}=I/e$ ion pairs s$^{-1}$

and the power put into the air in the volume which is actually
irradiated is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=N_{i}W=\frac{I\,W}{e}\;,
\end{equation*}
$W=33.97\;{\rm eV}$  per ion pair.  With $W_{J}=W\,e=5.44\times10^{-18}\,$J

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97=6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
in the volume which is seen by the beam.

At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\,{\rm mm^{2}}
      =1.80\times10^{-2}\;{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\end{equation*}
and with the chamber taken as the air column which is traversed by the
beam

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.80\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\qquad 
m_{air}= \rho_{air}V_{f}
       =1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272=3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}
       =3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
in the beam.  The air–kerma rate in the focus is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{I\,W/e}{m_{air}}
           =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
           \simeq 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $0.19~{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ of air kerma in the 0.30$\times$6
mm$^{2}$ microbeam.  With $\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{t}\simeq
\big(\mu_{en}/\rho\big)_{air}$ the same number is the tissue-kerma rate
in the microbeam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
 \approx0.69\;{\rm Gy\,h^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
in the stationary beam.

A material point of the object is under the 0.30 mm wide microbeam for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=\frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/t_{exp}}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}
per pass, the quantity quoted in the statement.  The dose actually
deposited in a point which is traversed once by the scanning microbeam
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{pt}= \dot K_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\approx
3.5\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}
per dwell, i.e. $\approx 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 3.9\times10^{-3}\ {\rm mGy}$
per 0.02 s exposure in the 33 keV Laue microbeam.  This is the
instantaneous entrance skin kerma in the beam; the quoted
$1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ is the conventional $0.19\;{\rm
mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air–kerma rate at the focus.

In the usual way the number is quoted as an instantaneous rate.  On the
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field which is covered by the scan the
integrated energy put into the whole field per complete scan is
$K_{pt}A_{mono}/A_{f}$.  With $A_{mono}/A_{f}=50/0.30=1.67\times10^{2}$ this
is

\begin{equation*}
K_{tot}\simeq K_{pt}\frac{A_{mono}}{A_{f}}\approx
3.9\times10^{-6}\times1.67\times10^{2}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
i.e. $\approx0.6\;{\rm mGy}$ of energy deposited in the 50 mm wide
field for one complete single–energy scan, the $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$
per point being the conventional entrance–surface dose quoted for
synchrotron microbeam multiple–energy imaging.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\times
6\,{\rm mm}$ incident size and the $0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ 
$\mu_{en}/\rho$ cancel in the kerma–rate expression, as expected, and
the quoted $\,t_{d}=0.02\;{\rm s}$ is the dwell time of a tissue point
under the $0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide microbeam.

public_text.7:
The air kerma rate which can be read off the ion chamber is

* $I=2.0\times10^{-12}\ {\rm A}$, $W_{air}\approx 33.97\;{\rm J/C}$,

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W_{air}
\end{equation*}

and for the air which is actually seen by the beam

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\ {\rm cm^{2}},\qquad
V_{f}=A_{f}L ,
\end{equation*}

$L=15.1\,{\rm cm}$ is the length of the chamber along the beam.  With
$\rho_{air}=1.293\;{\rm mg\;cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}A_{f}L=
1.293\times10^{-3}\ {\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.018\ {\rm cm^{2}}\times
15.1\ {\rm cm}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

the air-kerma rate at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{I\,W_{air}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
          \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}  ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\;h^{-1}}$ in air at the focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the absorbed-dose rate
in soft tissue at the focus is the same to a very good approximation,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\approx\dot K_{air}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,h^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $33\;{\rm keV}$ monochromatic beam.

In the scanning geometry a tissue element is illuminated only while the
$w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ beamlet passes over it.  With the quoted
$w_{f}/v$ dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}\approx\frac{w_{f}}{v}=0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dose actually deposited in the element in one passage through the
beamlet is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. of the order of a few $\mu$Gy per monochromatic exposure.  With
$W_{air}=33.97\;{\rm V}$ this is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx\frac{I\,W_{air}\,t_{d}}{ \rho_{air}A_{f}L}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\ \mu{\rm Gy\ per\;33\,keV\;pass}
\end{equation*}

in soft tissue, essentially the same as the air kerma delivered to the
surface.  In the usual dual- or triple-energy K-edge scheme the doses add
incoherently, so a two-energy acquisition is $\approx 2D_{1}\approx
7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ and a three-energy acquisition is
$\approx 3D_{1}\approx 1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}$ to the surface
that is scanned.  The often quoted $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV
micro-beam exposure is the conventional single-energy figure.

public_text.18:
The charge collected from the air in the chamber is a measure of the energy
which is absorbed in the air in the chamber.

For an air kerma

\begin{equation*}
K_{air}= \frac{Q}{m}\,\frac{W}{e}\approx
\frac{I}{m}\frac{W}{e}
\end{equation*}

with $W/e\simeq 33.97\,{\rm V}$ for air. The air mass which is
in the beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}= \rho_{air}~A_{f}L ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times 6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018\;{\rm cm^{2}} ,
\qquad L=15.1\;{\rm cm},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}\;{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times
0.018\times15.1\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\approx 3.5\times10^{-4}\;{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}\;{\rm kg} .
\end{equation*}

The power absorbed in the air in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
P_{a}= I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97\,{\rm V}
\approx 6.8\times10^{-11}\;{\rm W},
\end{equation*}

so that the peak air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{P_{a}}{m_{air}}
          =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{air}A_{f}L}
          \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
          \approx 1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
          \approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times 6\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot.  In terms of an energy
fluence

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi_{E}=\frac{P_{a}}{(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}m_{air}}
          \approx5.9\times10^{-7}\;{\rm J\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\Phi_{ph}\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}$ at
33 keV, $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$,
$E_{ph}=5.29\times10^{-15}\,{\rm J}$.

For tissue $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{air}$ the
dose rate at the surface in the focal spot is essentially the air
kerma rate above.

A point on the surface is seen by the $w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}$ wide
focal spot for the dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta x/\Delta t}
      \approx0.02\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

the $w_{f}/(\Delta x/\Delta t)$ which is quoted.  Hence the dose
deposited in a surface element during one pass of the monochromatic
beam is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\simeq \dot K_{air}\,t_{d}
      \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\;{\rm s}
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\;{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic projection, i.e. $\,\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 33 keV
exposure, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak in the focal spot.
In energy fluence terms this is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi_{E,1}= \dot\Phi_{E}t_{d}\approx1.2\times10^{-8}\;{\rm J\,cm^{-2}}
\approx2.2\times10^{6}\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per pass.  For an $n$-energy multiple–energy acquisition the
cumulative surface dose is $n\,D_{1}$, $\sim 8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
typical dual–energy acquisition and $\sim12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for three
energies, essentially the $3\!-\!4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy often
quoted for synchrotron scanning micro–CT.  This is the peak surface
dose; the mean dose over the scanned area is lower by the
$w_{f}/\Delta x$ overlap factor.

public_text.12:
For an air walled ion chamber

\begin{equation*}
{\dot H}_{air}= \frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}=\frac{I}{e}\frac{W}{e^{-1}}/
m_{air}
\end{equation*}

is the air kerma rate in the volume which is intercepted by the beam.
The volume which is intercepted when the chamber is on the focus is

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=w_{f}h_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
w_{f}=0.30\;{\rm mm}=3.0\times10^{-2}{\rm cm}\qquad 
h_{f}=6.0\;{\rm mm}=6.0\times10^{-1}{\rm cm}\qquad 
L_{c}=15.1\;{\rm cm}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.03\times0.60\times15.1=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{air}=V_{b}\rho_{air}=0.272\times1.293\times10^{-3}
       =3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm \;g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm \;kg} 
\end{equation*}

in the beam.  With the air  $W/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$,

\begin{equation*}
P_{abs}=\frac{I\,W}{e}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
       =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm \;W}
\end{equation*}

so that the instantaneous air kerma rate on the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}=\frac{P_{abs}}{m_{air}}
         =\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
         \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.019\;{\rm mGy\,min^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e.  about $ 1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy/s}$ in the 0.3$\times$6 mm
spot.  In the thin air limit

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{tissue}\simeq\dot K_{air}\;, \qquad 
\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big|_{tissue}\simeq\frac{\mu_{en}}{\rho}\Big|_{air},
\end{equation*}

so the entrance kerma rate in tissue is the same as the air kerma rate
in the chamber.

The sample is rastered horizontally.  The time a material point
spends inside the $w_{f}$ wide spot is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{scan}}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step}/{\rm exposure}}
\end{equation*}

and the data are given as

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=0.020\;{\rm s},
\end{equation*}

i.e. the conventional dwell time of a pixel in the 0.30 mm spot.
The kerma received by a material point during one 33 keV pass is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{tissue}\,t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic exposure, i.e. of the order

\[
K_{1}\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}\approx3.9\times10^{-4}{\rm rad}
\]

entrance kerma at the surface for the 33 keV projection.  In air
kerma units this is $ \approx 3.9\times10^{-6}$ J kg$^{-1}$ per
exposure.  With the usual $W_{air}=33.97$ eV this is the $\,I\,W/e$
energy deposited in the $0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}}$ of air in the beam
during $t_{d}$.

For a multi-energy set the surface kerma adds incoherently.  For e.g.
$N_{E}$ energies of comparable flux the cumulative entrance kerma is
$K_{\rm cum}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}$, i.e. $\sim 8$–$12\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the
two- to three-energy acquisitions normally used in the Laue
microprobe.  This is the familiar $\mu$Gy–level synchrotron
micro-tomography entrance dose.  The time-averaged dose over the
$w_{M}\times h_{M}=50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ field which is scanned
with the $w_{f}\times h_{f}$ spot is $K_{1}w_{f}/w_{M}\approx
2.3\times10^{-8}\ {\rm Gy}$ per pass, as usual for a raster-scanned
microbeam.

public_text.10:
For an air ionisation chamber the energy which is put into the air is

* $E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e$

with $W/e\simeq 33.9\;{\rm J/C}$ for air.  For the measurement quoted

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{\rm dep}=I\,W/e =2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times 33.97\,{\rm J/C}
      \simeq 6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}
in the air which is actually intersected by the beam.  With the beam
$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ $h_{f}=6.0\,{\rm mm}$ at the focus the air which is
seen by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
V_{a}=w_{f}h_{f}L=
0.03\,{\rm cm}\times0.60\,{\rm cm}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{a}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g/cm^{3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
      \simeq 3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}\;,
\end{equation*}

so that the air-kerma rate which is produced in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{\rm dep}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}w_{f}h_{f}L}
      \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
      \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma at the focus.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ this is the
tissue-kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ pencil beam.
In the usual scanning notation the dwell time of a material point in the
pencil is

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=\frac{w_{f}}{\Delta s/\Delta t}\approx0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

so that the dose actually received by a material point on a single
Laue channel is

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{a}\,t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times0.020
      \approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\,{\rm \sim 3\!-\!4\;\mu Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$ exposure,
$\,\approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous kerma in the beam.
This is the conventional ‘single-energy’ synchrotron pencil-beam dose
often quoted for the system.

If the dose is summed over the whole Laue fan which is scanned in the
usual multiple-energy mode the $50\,{\rm mm}$ source width is imaged
onto the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focus.  The number of independent Laue
channels which are stepped through in a complete horizontal scan is

\begin{equation*}
N_{E}\simeq\frac{W_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2},
\end{equation*}

so that the total kerma delivered to a surface element which is taken
through the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ fan is

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm tot}\simeq N_{E}K_{1}\approx
1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

$\approx 0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$ cumulative surface kerma for the
$\approx 3.3\;{\rm s}$ $50\;{\rm mm}$ multi-energy scan, i.e. $\approx
3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per Laue energy step, $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous in the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm}$ beam.  The $3.9\;\mu{\rm
Gy}$ per $0.02\;{\rm s}$ dwell is the usual single-channel figure which
is quoted as $\,\sim 4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per image.  With the often quoted
$W/e=33.85\;{\rm J/C}$ the numbers become $K_{1}\approx3.86\,\mu{\rm Gy}$
and $K_{\rm tot}\approx0.64\;{\rm mGy}$ for the full 167-channel fan,
essentially the same result.

public_text.16:
For an air filled ionisation chamber the charge which is liberated per
unit absorbed energy is $e/W_{air}$ with

* $W_{air}/e\simeq 33.97\;{\rm V}$

so that the power which is deposited in the gas in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =\frac{W_{air}}{e}I .
\end{equation*}

With $I=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot E =33.97\times2.0\times10^{-12}=6.79\times10^{-11}\;{\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
is the power which is absorbed in the air which is at any instant in
the 33.0 keV focal spot.

The air which is in the spot at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}= w_{f}h_{f}L_{c}
\end{equation*}

$w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}=3.0\times10^{-2}{\rm cm}$,
$h_{f}=6.0\,{\rm mm}=6.0\times10^{-1}{\rm cm}$ and
$L_{c}=15.1\,{\rm cm}$, so that

\begin{equation*}
V_{b}=0.018\times15.1=2.72\times10^{-1}{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

and with $\rho_{air}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}$

\begin{equation*}
m_{b}=\rho_{air}V_{b}=3.5\!-\!3.6\times10^{-4}\ {\rm g}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is in the focal spot.  The air kerma rate in
the spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{air}= \frac{\dot E}{m_{b}}
          =\frac{I\,W_{air}/e}{m_{b}}
          \simeq\frac{6.79\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-4}}
          \approx1.9\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx 0.19\;\mu{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$, an energy–absorption
mass–attenuation coefficient of air of $0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at
33 keV being essentially that of soft tissue.  The photon fluence rate
which corresponds to this kerma rate is

\begin{equation*}
\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{air}}{E\,\mu_{en}/\rho}
      \approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-7}}
                   {5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.328}
      \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}} ,
\end{equation*}

in the focal spot.

A material point is in the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide focal spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
      \frac{w_{f}}{{\rm scan\;step/exposure}}
      \approx 2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s} ,
\end{equation*}

the time quoted in the statement.  The air kerma, and to a very good
approximation the absorbed dose in tissue, received by a point which
passes once through the spot is therefore

\begin{equation*}
K_{1}= \dot K_{air}\;t_{d}
     \approx1.93\times10^{-7}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3\!-\!4\times10^{-9}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per single–energy projection, i.e. a few nGy per projection, $ \approx
3.9\,{\rm nGy}$ for the numbers quoted.  In energy-fluence terms this is

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{1}= \Phi t_{d}E\approx1.2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per projection, $K_{1}= \Psi_{1}\mu_{en}/\rho\approx3.9\times10^{-9}\ {\rm
Gy}$.  For the two or three energies normally used in a Laue multiple-
energy tomography the dose adds linearly, e.g. $\approx 7\!-\!12\,{\rm
nGy}$ for two–three energies.

If the dose is referred to the $50\,{\rm mm}$ entrance field which is
imaged by scanning the $0.30\,{\rm mm}$ focal spot,

\begin{equation*}
N_{p}\approx\frac{50}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

steps are required.  The integrated energy deposited in the whole
$50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$ field per single-energy scan is

\begin{equation*}
K_{field}\approx N_{p}K_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-9}
          \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-7}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per energy, essentially the average kerma which is delivered to the
scanned tissue.  The $4\,{\rm nGy}$ per projection is the conventional
single-point cumulative surface dose; the $\sim0.6\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ 
figure is the integrated dose over the 50 mm field of view which is
normally quoted as the cumulative surface dose of the scan.  The
$\sim2\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ figure often quoted after the $L_{c}/w_{f}\approx
503$ averaging correction for the 15.1 cm scanning ion chamber is
$K_{1}^{\rm pk}\approx1.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy projection, i.e.
$\approx 1\!-\!2\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per projection for the usual 2–3 energy
set, the few-nGy number above being the dose per point in the
un-corrected, stationary-chamber limit.

3.text:
For an air walled chamber the ionisation power in the air which is
actually in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W_{a}/e\approx2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  The mass of air which is in the $0.30\times6.0{\rm\,
mm^{2}}$ spot at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the fully illuminated part of the
chamber, i.e. the peak kerma rate in the $0.30\times6$ mm pencil
beam, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
   \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
   \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\,\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma in the focal spot.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq \dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the dose rate in soft tissue in the beam and

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi=
\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx
\frac{1.93\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
\approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}\approx1.1\times10^{8}
\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.

A surface element is covered by the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}\Big/({\rm step/t_{exp}}\Big)\approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted for the scan.  The kerma actually received by a
material point on one monochromatic pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.7\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV Laue micro-beam dwell, $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ entrance air kerma and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ absorbed surface dose in
soft tissue per energy.  In fluence terms

$D_{1}= \Phi\,\!t_{d}E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}\approx2.2\times10^{10}
\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}$ per 20 ms dwell.

The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal demagnification is
already contained in the 2.0 pA measured at the focus.  The doses of
the different Laue energies add incoherently.  For the usual
$N_{E}$-energy set

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\,,
\end{equation*}

e.g. $\,D_{2}\approx2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a dual-energy
acquisition and

\begin{equation*}
D_{3}\approx3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

for the conventional three-energy K-edge subtraction set, $\approx
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per individual 33 keV energy.  Integrated over the
whole $W_{0}=50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan $N_{ch}\simeq W_{0}/w_{f}\approx167$
independent channels give a total energy deposited in the
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field per single energy

$K_{\rm field}\simeq N_{ch}D_{1}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}$

per complete single-energy scan, the $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point
being the conventional peak surface kerma quoted for synchrotron
scanning micro-beam multiple-energy imaging. ∎

7.text:
In the gas which is actually intercepted by the beam

* $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power put into ionisation in the irradiated air.

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018~{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}=0.272~{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\,
{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
of air is in the beam at any instant. Hence the air kerma rate in the
$0.30\times6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}$ pencil at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}w_{f}h_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same rate is the
entrance kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}{\rm \;Gy\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\dot\Phi\approx\frac{\dot D_{t}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}
        \approx1.1\times10^{8}{\rm \;ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV in the focal spot.

For a raster scan the time a material point spends in the
$w_{f}=0.30~{\rm mm}$ wide pencil is the quoted dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step/exposure}}\approx0.020\;{\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the dose received by a surface element in one monochromatic
Laue exposure is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e.

$\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per 20 ms dwell,
$ \approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the $0.30\times 6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
beam, per Laue energy.  In fluence terms this is $\approx2.2\times10^{10}\,
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}$ per dwell.

The dose is additive with energy.  For the conventional two-energy
K-edge subtraction $N_{E}=2$

\begin{equation*}
D_{2}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
and for three energies

\begin{equation*}
D_{3}\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic $33\;{\rm keV}$ pass being the
normally quoted synchrotron micro-beam surface dose.  The $50\;{\rm mm}\to
0.30\;{\rm mm}$ demagnification is already contained in the measured
$2.0\;{\rm pA}$ focal current.  On an average-over-field basis

$N_{p}=W_{0}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ Laue channels
give an integrated single-energy kerma over the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
fan

$K_{tot}\simeq N_{p}D_{1}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$,
i.e. $\approx D_{1}w_{f}/W_{0}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}$ mean
over the monochromator field, as usual for a raster scanned microbeam.

The $\sim\!n{\rm Gy}$ numbers obtained by keeping the air mass in kg
as $3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm kg}$ instead of $3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}$ are
a $10^{3}$ unit conversion error.  With the correct mass the
$\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\sim0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak, result
is recovered. ∎

6.text:
An air chamber gives directly the air kerma in the air which is in the
beam at the focus.

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\frac{W}{e}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          \simeq6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm J\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the power put into ionisation in the air which is intersected by the
$0.30\times 6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal spot.  The air mass which is in the
beam at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30{\rm mm}\times6.0{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},
\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the peak air kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}\approx
\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}\approx
1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the entrance kerma/dose rate in soft tissue in the $0.30\times6.0\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
pencil beam, i.e.

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi =\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}\approx
1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}},\qquad
E=33{\rm keV}=5.29\times10^{-15}{\rm J},
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal
demagnification of the Laue fan is already contained in the measured
$2.0\,{\rm pA}$ focal current.

A material point is in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide pencil for the
dwell time

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step/exposure}}\approx0.02\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

quoted for the horizontal raster.  Hence the dose actually received by
a surface element in one monochromatic pass is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}\ {\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

about $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $20\,{\rm ms}$ dwell per 33 keV Laue
energy, i.e. $\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ spot.  In fluence terms

$D_{1}\approx\Phi t_{d}E(\mu_{en}/\rho)\approx2.2\times10^{10}\ {\rm
ph\,cm^{-2}}$ per dwell.

This is the conventional synchrotron micro-beam single-energy entrance
dose.  Doses add incoherently for the different monochromator energies.
For the usual dual-energy acquisition

\begin{equation*}
D_{2}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

and for a three-energy K-edge set

\begin{equation*}
D_{3}\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
11\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per imaged surface element, i.e. $\sim4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic
energy, $\sim10\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ cumulative for a typical multiple-energy
scan.  The $N_{E}=W_{0}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$
Laue channels which make up the $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm mm}$
monochromator footprint give a field integrated kerma per single energy
scan of

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm field}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per complete $50\,{\rm mm}$ raster, the $3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 20 ms
dwell being the peak entrance surface dose actually delivered to the
tissue which is instantaneously in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
focal spot.  This is the $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ level normally quoted for
synchrotron Laue multiple-energy micro-imaging. ∎

11.text:
The charge which is liberated in the air which is actually in the beam is

* $\dot E_{a}=I\,W/e$ ,  $W/e\simeq 33.97\,{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the air which is intersected by the focussed pencil.  At the focus

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}
     =1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},\qquad 
V_{f}=A_{f}L=1.8\times10^{-2}\times15.1=0.272{\rm cm^{3}} ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272{\rm cm^{3}}
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

is the mass of air which is irradiated at the peak of the scan.  The
instantaneous air-kerma rate in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ pencil
is therefore

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}w_{f}h_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\approx0.19\ {\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in air.  With $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\ {\rm
cm^{2}g^{-1}}$ at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the absorbed-dose rate in soft tissue while the point is fully in
the beam.  In fluence terms

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi=\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}
       \approx\frac{1.93\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
       \approx1.1\times10^{8}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}\;,
\end{equation*}

in the focal spot.

A material point stays in the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide pencil for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}\equiv\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step/exposure}}
      \approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted in the experiment.  The kerma absorbed by one
surface element in one Laue energy pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.6\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e. 

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.8-4.0\ \mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\ \mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

per monochromatic 33 keV exposure, $ \approx0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$
instantaneous in the $0.30\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$ beam.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ this is the entrance
absorbed surface dose in tissue.  The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$
demagnification of the Laue fan is already contained in the measured
$I=2.0\,{\rm pA}$ at the focus.

In the usual multiple energy usage the doses add incoherently,

\begin{equation*}
D_{N_{E}}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\;,
\end{equation*}

e.g. $D_{2}\approx2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for the classic
dual-energy acquisition and $D_{3}\approx3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times
10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a three-energy K-edge
set.  The often quoted single-energy, per-point, synchrotron micro-beam
figure is the $\sim3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}\approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per $0.02\,{\rm s}$
dwell quoted above, $ \approx1.9\times10^{-4}\,{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}$ peak in
the focal spot.  The integrated kerma over the whole $W_{0}=50\,{\rm mm}$
horizontal Laue fan is $N_{p}=W_{0}/w_{f}\approx167$ times larger,
$K_{{\rm tot}}\approx N_{p}D_{1}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\,{\rm mGy}$ per
single-energy full fan scan, the $ \approx4\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ being the
conventional peak surface dose per imaged pixel.

16.text:
In an air walled chamber the power which is put into the air in the
volume which is traversed by the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}= I\,\frac{W}{e}\simeq2.0\times10^{-12}\times33.97
       \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

in the volume which is instantaneously in the $0.30\times6.0\,{\rm mm^{2}}$
focal spot.  With $L=15.1\;{\rm cm}$

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.8\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}},\qquad
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.272\;{\rm cm^{3}},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}=\rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272
     \approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

of air is irradiated at the peak of the scan, so that the peak air
kerma rate in the focal spot is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.5\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\dot K_{a}\simeq0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air at the focus.
With

\begin{equation*}
\Big(\mu_{en}/\rho\Big)_{t}\simeq\Big(\mu_{en}/\rho\Big)_{a}=0.328\;
{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

at $33\,{\rm keV}$ this is the tissue kerma rate in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm
mm^{2}}$ pencil beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}},
\qquad 
\dot\Phi\approx\dot K_{a}/E(\mu_{en}/\rho)\approx1.1\times10^{8}\;
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}} .
\end{equation*}

A material point stays under the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide pencil for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}\equiv w_{f}/({\rm step}/{\rm exposure})\approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

as quoted.  The kerma – and, with the $\mu_{en}/\rho$ equivalence, the
absorbed dose – actually received by the point in one monochromatic
pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.6\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e. $\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per 0.02 s dwell per 33 keV Laue channel,
$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the focal spot.  In
photon terms

\begin{equation*}
\Psi_{1}= \dot\Phi t_{d}E\approx1.2\times10^{-8}\ {\rm J\,cm^{-2}}
      \approx2.2\times10^{9}\ {\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}
\end{equation*}

per dwell.  With the usual $W/e=33.97\,{\rm V}$ this is $D_{1}\approx
3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\approx7\!-\!8\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
conventional dual–energy acquisition and $\approx3D_{1}\approx
1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\,{\rm Gy}\approx10\!-\!12\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a
three–energy K-edge subtraction set, the familiar $\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$
level synchrotron micro–beam entrance dose.

The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal demagnification is
already contained in the measured $2.0\,{\rm pA}$ at the focus.  In
number of independent Laue channels

\begin{equation*}
N_{p}\simeq\frac{W_{0}}{w_{f}}=\frac{50.0}{0.30}\approx1.67\times10^{2}
\end{equation*}

the integrated kerma delivered to the whole $50\,{\rm mm}\times6\,{\rm
mm}$ fan in a complete single–energy scan is

\begin{equation*}
K_{\rm fan}\simeq N_{p}D_{1}\approx1.67\times10^{2}\times3.9\times10^{-6}
      \approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV fan scan, $\approx0.64\,{\rm mGy}$ with $W/e=33.85\,{\rm V}$,
i.e. $D_{1}\approx3.9\,\mu{\rm Gy}$ per dwell is the conventional
single–projection, single–pixel surface dose normally quoted for the
system.  The $0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak rate is the dose rate in the
$0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focal spot. ∎

public_text.3:
For an air walled chamber the ionisation power in the air which is
actually in the beam is

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{a}=I\,W_{a}/e\approx2.0\times10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
        \approx6.8\times10^{-11}\ {\rm W}
\end{equation*}

at the focus.  The mass of air which is in the $0.30\times6.0{\rm\,
mm^{2}}$ spot at any instant is

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=1.80\times10^{-2}{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L=0.018\times15.1=0.272\ {\rm cm^{3}},\qquad
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}\times0.272\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}
      =3.5\times10^{-7}\ {\rm kg}
\end{equation*}

so that the air kerma rate in the fully illuminated part of the
chamber, i.e. the peak kerma rate in the $0.30\times6$ mm pencil
beam, is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}= \frac{\dot E_{a}}{m_{a}}=
\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}A_{f}L}
   \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
   \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\,\approx 0.19\,{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ air kerma in the focal spot.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq (\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}=0.328\;{\rm cm^{2}g^{-1}}$
at 33 keV

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq \dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

is the dose rate in soft tissue in the beam and

\begin{equation*}
\dot\Phi=
\frac{\dot K_{a}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}}\approx
\frac{1.93\times10^{-4}}{5.29\times10^{-15}\times0.0328}
\approx1.1\times10^{12}\ {\rm m^{-2}s^{-1}}\approx1.1\times10^{8}
\ {\rm cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

in the $0.30\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ focus.

A surface element is covered by the $w_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}$ wide spot for

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}=w_{f}/v_{s}=w_{f}\Big/({\rm step/t_{exp}}\Big)\approx0.020\ {\rm s}
\end{equation*}

the dwell time quoted for the scan.  The kerma actually received by a
material point on one monochromatic pass is therefore

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot K_{a}t_{d}\approx1.93\times10^{-4}\times2.0\times10^{-2}
     \approx3.7\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}\ {\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

i.e.

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}\times0.02\;{\rm s}
\end{equation*}

per 33 keV Laue micro-beam dwell, $\approx 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx
0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ entrance air kerma and, with $(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\approx
(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$, $\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ absorbed surface dose in
soft tissue per energy.  In fluence terms

$D_{1}= \Phi\,\!t_{d}E(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}\approx2.2\times10^{10}
\;{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}$ per 20 ms dwell.

The $50\,{\rm mm}\to0.30\,{\rm mm}$ horizontal demagnification is
already contained in the 2.0 pA measured at the focus.  The doses of
the different Laue energies add incoherently.  For the usual
$N_{E}$-energy set

\begin{equation*}
D_{N}\simeq N_{E}D_{1}\,,
\end{equation*}

e.g. $\,D_{2}\approx2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ for a dual-energy
acquisition and

\begin{equation*}
D_{3}\approx3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}\ {\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

for the conventional three-energy K-edge subtraction set, $\approx
4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per individual 33 keV energy.  Integrated over the
whole $W_{0}=50\,{\rm mm}$ Laue fan $N_{ch}\simeq W_{0}/w_{f}\approx167$
independent channels give a total energy deposited in the
$50\times6\,{\rm mm^{2}}$ field per single energy

$K_{\rm field}\simeq N_{ch}D_{1}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}\ {\rm Gy}
\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\ {\rm mGy}$

per complete single-energy scan, the $ \sim 4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per point
being the conventional peak surface kerma quoted for synchrotron
scanning micro-beam multiple-energy imaging. ∎

public_text.7:
In the gas which is actually intercepted by the beam

* $W/e=33.97\;{\rm V}$ for air

\begin{equation*}
\dot E_{air}=I\,W/e =2.0\times 10^{-12}{\rm A}\times33.97{\rm V}
          =6.8\times10^{-11}{\rm W}
\end{equation*}
is the power put into ionisation in the irradiated air.

\begin{equation*}
A_{f}=w_{f}h_{f}=0.30\,{\rm mm}\times6.0\,{\rm mm}=0.018~{\rm cm^{2}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
V_{f}=A_{f}L =0.018\,{\rm cm^{2}}\times15.1\,{\rm cm}=0.272~{\rm cm^{3}}
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
m_{a}= \rho_{a}V_{f}=1.293\times10^{-3}{\rm g\,cm^{-3}}\times0.272\,
{\rm cm^{3}}\approx3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm g}=3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}
\end{equation*}
of air is in the beam at any instant. Hence the air kerma rate in the
$0.30\times6.0~{\rm mm^{2}}$ pencil at the focus is

\begin{equation*}
\dot K_{a}=\frac{\dot E_{air}}{m_{a}}
        =\frac{I\,W/e}{\rho_{a}w_{f}h_{f}L}
        \approx\frac{6.8\times10^{-11}}{3.52\times10^{-7}}
        \approx1.9\!-\!2.0\times10^{-4}\;{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}

$\approx0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ in air.  With
$(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{t}\simeq(\mu_{en}/\rho)_{a}$ the same rate is the
entrance kerma / absorbed dose rate in soft tissue in the beam,

\begin{equation*}
\dot D_{t}\simeq\dot K_{a}\approx1.9\times10^{-4}{\rm \;Gy\,s^{-1}},\qquad
\dot\Phi\approx\frac{\dot D_{t}}{E(\mu_{en}/\rho)}
        \approx1.1\times10^{8}{\rm \;ph\,cm^{-2}s^{-1}}
\end{equation*}
at 33 keV in the focal spot.

For a raster scan the time a material point spends in the
$w_{f}=0.30~{\rm mm}$ wide pencil is the quoted dwell

\begin{equation*}
t_{d}= \frac{w_{f}}{v_{s}}=
\frac{w_{f}}{{\rm step/exposure}}\approx0.020\;{\rm s}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that the dose received by a surface element in one monochromatic
Laue exposure is

\begin{equation*}
D_{1}= \dot D_{t}\,t_{d}\approx
1.93\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy\,s^{-1}}\times2.0\times10^{-2}{\rm s}
\approx3.8\!-\!4.0\times10^{-6}{\rm Gy}
\end{equation*}

i.e.

$\approx3.9\;\mu{\rm Gy}\approx0.4\;{\rm mrad}$ per 20 ms dwell,
$ \approx 0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ instantaneous in the $0.30\times 6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
beam, per Laue energy.  In fluence terms this is $\approx2.2\times10^{10}\,
{\rm ph\,cm^{-2}}$ per dwell.

The dose is additive with energy.  For the conventional two-energy
K-edge subtraction $N_{E}=2$

\begin{equation*}
D_{2}\simeq2D_{1}\approx7\!-\!8\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}
and for three energies

\begin{equation*}
D_{3}\simeq3D_{1}\approx1.1\!-\!1.2\times10^{-5}{\rm Gy}\approx
10\!-\!12\;\mu{\rm Gy},
\end{equation*}

$\approx4\;\mu{\rm Gy}$ per monochromatic $33\;{\rm keV}$ pass being the
normally quoted synchrotron micro-beam surface dose.  The $50\;{\rm mm}\to
0.30\;{\rm mm}$ demagnification is already contained in the measured
$2.0\;{\rm pA}$ focal current.  On an average-over-field basis

$N_{p}=W_{0}/w_{f}\approx50/0.30\approx1.67\times10^{2}$ Laue channels
give an integrated single-energy kerma over the $50\times6\;{\rm mm^{2}}$
fan

$K_{tot}\simeq N_{p}D_{1}\approx6\!-\!7\times10^{-4}{\rm Gy}\approx0.6\!-\!0.7\;{\rm mGy}$,
i.e. $\approx D_{1}w_{f}/W_{0}\approx2\!-\!3\times10^{-8}{\rm Gy}$ mean
over the monochromator field, as usual for a raster scanned microbeam.

The $\sim\!n{\rm Gy}$ numbers obtained by keeping the air mass in kg
as $3.5\times10^{-4}{\rm kg}$ instead of $3.5\times10^{-7}{\rm kg}$ are
a $10^{3}$ unit conversion error.  With the correct mass the
$\sim\mu{\rm Gy}$ per energy, $\sim0.19\;{\rm mGy\,s^{-1}}$ peak, result
is recovered. ∎

public_text.6:
An air chamber gives directly the air kerma in the air which is in the
